{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the equation \\(z^5 + z^4 + 2z^3 + z^2 + z = 0\\), we start by factoring out the common term \\(z\\):\n\n\\[z(z^4 + z^3 + 2z^2 + z + 1) = 0.\\]\n\nThis gives us one solution immediately:\n\n\\[z = 0.\\]\n\nNext, we need to solve the equation \\(z^4 + z^3 + 2z^2 + z + 1 = 0\\). To do this, we will check if there are any obvious roots or use other algebraic methods. One useful approach is to test possible roots using the Rational Root Theorem, but since the coefficients are not integers, we will use another method.\n\nWe can try to find the roots of the polynomial \\(z^4 + z^3 + 2z^2 + z + 1\\) by considering possible roots on the unit circle. Let's assume \\(z = e^{i\\theta}\\) and substitute it into the polynomial:\n\n\\[e^{4i\\theta} + e^{3i\\theta} + 2e^{2i\\theta} + e^{i\\theta} + 1 = 0.\\]\n\nWe can test specific values of \\(\\theta\\) to see if they satisfy the equation. Let's test \\(\\theta = \\frac{2\\pi}{5}\\):\n\n","prefix_end":902,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,23606,17767,89,61,20,488,1147,61,19,488,220,17,89,61,18,488,1147,61,17,488,1147,284,220,15,59,701,582,1191,553,2097,5503,700,279,4185,4647,17767,89,59,7731,78045,89,13174,61,19,488,1147,61,18,488,220,17,89,61,17,488,1147,488,220,16,8,284,220,15,7110,2533,1986,6696,601,825,6291,7069,1447,78045,89,284,220,15,7110,2533,5847,11,582,1184,311,11625,279,23606,17767,89,61,19,488,1147,61,18,488,220,17,89,61,17,488,1147,488,220,16,284,220,15,59,568,2014,653,419,11,582,686,1779,421,1052,525,894,8036,19703,476,990,1008,46876,292,5413,13,3776,5390,5486,374,311,1273,3204,19703,1667,279,54525,18854,576,13173,11,714,2474,279,36829,525,537,25780,11,582,686,990,2441,1714,382,1654,646,1430,311,1477,279,19703,315,279,47311,17767,89,61,19,488,1147,61,18,488,220,17,89,61,17,488,1147,488,220,16,57758,553,12831,3204,19703,389,279,4982,1267